<div class="help-block" style="font-size:13px">
<p>Add a rule for each exception. With no matching rule, the section stays visible.</p>
<details style="margin-bottom:12px">
<summary style="cursor:pointer">How rules work</summary>
<div style="margin-top:8px">
<p>Choose <strong>What to check → Comparison → Values</strong>, then select <strong>Show, Hide or Read-only</strong> on that same row.</p>
<p>To target a page, choose <strong>Action</strong> and select <strong>Add</strong>, <strong>Edit</strong> or both as values. Without conditions, the rule always applies.</p>
<p>Use AND or OR between rows. AND-linked rows must all match; OR starts an alternative group. Among matching groups, Hide takes priority over Read-only, then Show. Multiple selected values within one condition mean any of those values.</p>
<p>Rules run from top to bottom; the first match wins. Move specific rules above general rules. For example, allow a department with a Visible rule, followed by an unconditional Read-only rule for everyone else.</p>
<p>Logged-in user conditions check the person using the form. Record conditions check form values. Approval process: Current step checks the active approval step; on Add, this is the first step. Existing permissions and restrictions on the containing tab still apply.</p>
</div>
</details>
<p>Click <strong>Save Rules &amp; Apply</strong>. Enter the parent form password when asked; it unlocks the module for 30 minutes. The module rebuilds automatically after saving.</p>
</div>
